(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("ai-infra",{"ai-infra/gpu-scheduling-and-sharing-on-kubernetes":{"html":"\u003cp>GPUs are scarce and expensive, and a default pod gets a whole one even if it uses a tenth. Good scheduling and sharing decide how much useful work each GPU hour buys.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The device plugin and extended resources\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Whole GPU, time slicing, MIG and MPS\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Taints, node selectors and topology\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Queueing and gang scheduling for training\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Monitoring utilisation\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"device-plugin\">Device plugin\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The NVIDIA device plugin advertises \u003ccode>nvidia.com/gpu\u003c/code> as an extended resource. A pod asks for it in limits and the scheduler places it on a node with free GPUs. GPUs are not overcommitted by default, so one pod requesting one GPU holds the whole device.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sharing\">Sharing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Time slicing lets several pods share a GPU in turns with no memory isolation, which suits light inference and development. MIG on supported cards partitions a GPU into isolated slices with their own memory and compute. MPS lets processes share compute concurrently. Choose by isolation needs and by what your cards support.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"placement\">Placement\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Taint GPU nodes so that only pods that tolerate the taint land on them, and use node selectors or affinity for a card type. For multi GPU jobs, topology matters: GPUs connected by a fast interconnect on the same node perform much better than GPUs across nodes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"queues-and-gangs\">Queues and gangs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A distributed training job needs all its workers at once. Without gang scheduling, half the workers start and wait while holding GPUs. Queue systems such as Kueue or Volcano admit a job only when all resources are available and share quota between teams.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"monitoring\">Monitoring\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Export DCGM metrics for GPU utilisation, memory and power. A GPU that is allocated but idle is the most common waste. Alert on allocation with low utilisation over hours and report it per team.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"job&gt;sch;sch&gt;node;node&gt;mon\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">GPU scheduling and sharing on Kubernetes\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"job\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pod requesting GPU\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sch\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Scheduler and queue\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"node\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">GPU node\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mon\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">DCGM metrics\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Pod requesting GPU to Scheduler and queue; 2, Scheduler and queue to GPU node; 3, GPU node to DCGM metrics.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"job\">\u003cdt>Pod requesting GPU \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Asks for devices.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Whole GPU for little work.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sch\">\u003cdt>Scheduler and queue \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Places and admits.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Partial gang.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"node\">\u003cdt>GPU node \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Device plugin and slices.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Idle device.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mon\">\u003cdt>DCGM metrics \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Utilisation.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not watched.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-gpus-allocated-and-idle\">Incident: GPUs allocated and idle\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Eight GPUs were reserved for a notebook and used less than five percent for a week.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl exec dcgm-exporter-x -- curl -s localhost:9400/metrics | grep DCGM_FI_DEV_GPU_UTIL | head -2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> dcgm-exporter-x -- curl -s localhost:9400/metrics | grep DCGM_FI_DEV_GPU_UTIL | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">DCGM_FI_DEV_GPU_UTIL{gpu=&quot;0&quot;,pod=&quot;nb-ana&quot;} 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">DCGM_FI_DEV_GPU_UTIL{gpu=&quot;1&quot;,pod=&quot;nb-ana&quot;} 0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The notebook held its GPUs between experiments. The team moved light work to time sliced GPUs, added an idle culler that releases notebooks after two hours and published per team utilisation so owners could see the waste.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Giving whole GPUs to light workloads\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No taints so CPU pods land on GPU nodes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Starting partial training jobs without gang scheduling\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mixing isolation levels without understanding them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never watching utilisation\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"device-plugin","text":"Device plugin"},{"id":"sharing","text":"Sharing"},{"id":"placement","text":"Placement"},{"id":"queues-and-gangs","text":"Queues and gangs"},{"id":"monitoring","text":"Monitoring"},{"id":"incident-gpus-allocated-and-idle","text":"Incident: GPUs allocated and idle"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Schedule GPUs","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/manage-gpus/scheduling-gpus/"},{"t":"Dynamic resource allocation","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/dynamic-resource-allocation/"},{"t":"Multi-Instance GPU guide","u":"https://docs.nvidia.com/datacenter/tesla/mig-user-guide/"}],"text":"GPUs are scarce and expensive, and a default pod gets a whole one even if it uses a tenth. Good scheduling and sharing decide how much useful work each GPU hour buys. What you will learn The device plugin and extended resources Whole GPU, time slicing, MIG and MPS Taints, node selectors and topology Queueing and gang scheduling for training Monitoring utilisation Device plugin The NVIDIA device plugin advertises nvidia.com/gpu as an extended resource. A pod asks for it in limits and the scheduler places it on a node with free GPUs. GPUs are not overcommitted by default, so one pod requesting one GPU holds the whole device. Sharing Time slicing lets several pods share a GPU in turns with no memory isolation, which suits light inference and development. MIG on supported cards partitions a GPU into isolated slices with their own memory and compute. MPS lets processes share compute concurrently. Choose by isolation needs and by what your cards support. Placement Taint GPU nodes so that only pods that tolerate the taint land on them, and use node selectors or affinity for a card type. For multi GPU jobs, topology matters: GPUs connected by a fast interconnect on the same node perform much better than GPUs across nodes. Queues and gangs A distributed training job needs all its workers at once. Without gang scheduling, half the workers start and wait while holding GPUs. Queue systems such as Kueue or Volcano admit a job only when all resources are available and share quota between teams. Monitoring Export DCGM metrics for GPU utilisation, memory and power. A GPU that is allocated but idle is the most common waste. Alert on allocation with low utilisation over hours and report it per team. GPU scheduling and sharing on Kubernetes Flow in order: 1, Pod requesting GPU to Scheduler and queue; 2, Scheduler and queue to GPU node; 3, GPU node to DCGM metrics. Service Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Pod requesting GPU Service What it does: Asks for devices. If it fails or is weak: Whole GPU for little work. Scheduler and queue Step What it does: Places and admits. If it fails or is weak: Partial gang. GPU node Service What it does: Device plugin and slices. If it fails or is weak: Idle device. DCGM metrics Observability What it does: Utilisation. If it fails or is weak: Not watched. Incident: GPUs allocated and idle Incident Eight GPUs were reserved for a notebook and used less than five percent for a week. $ kubectl exec dcgm-exporter-x -- curl -s localhost:9400/metrics | grep DCGM_FI_DEV_GPU_UTIL | head -2 DCGM_FI_DEV_GPU_UTIL{gpu=\"0\",pod=\"nb-ana\"} 3 DCGM_FI_DEV_GPU_UTIL{gpu=\"1\",pod=\"nb-ana\"} 0 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The notebook held its GPUs between experiments. The team moved light work to time sliced GPUs, added an idle culler that releases notebooks after two hours and published per team utilisation so owners could see the waste. Common mistakes Giving whole GPUs to light workloads No taints so CPU pods land on GPU nodes Starting partial training jobs without gang scheduling Mixing isolation levels without understanding them Never watching utilisation"},"ai-infra/vector-databases-and-retrieval-operations":{"html":"\u003cp>Retrieval systems find text by meaning using vectors. Operating one is a database problem with new trade-offs between speed, memory and accuracy.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Embeddings and similarity search\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Index types: flat, IVF and HNSW\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sizing memory and disk\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Filtering, updates and consistency\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Measuring recall, latency and cost\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"embeddings\">Embeddings\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An embedding model turns text into a vector of a few hundred to a few thousand numbers. Texts with similar meaning have vectors that are close under a measure such as cosine similarity. Changing the embedding model changes every vector, so all stored data must be re-embedded.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"index-types\">Index types\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A flat index compares the query to every vector and is exact but slow at scale. Approximate indexes such as HNSW graphs or IVF clusters trade a little accuracy for much lower latency. HNSW is fast and memory heavy, IVF uses less memory and needs training on your data.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sizing\">Sizing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A vector of 1536 floats takes about six kilobytes, so ten million vectors need roughly sixty gigabytes before the index overhead. Quantisation reduces size at some accuracy cost. Keep hot indexes in memory and plan for replicas for availability and read load.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"filters-and-updates\">Filters and updates\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Most queries filter by metadata, such as tenant or date. Filtering after the search can return too few results, and filtering before can slow the search, so test your engine with realistic filters. Frequent updates and deletes may need background compaction or index rebuilds.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"measuring\">Measuring\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Recall is the share of the true nearest neighbours that the index returns. Measure it against an exact search on a sample, together with latency at the 95th and 99th percentile. Tune index parameters to meet a recall target at the lowest cost, and re-test after growth.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"emb&gt;idx;idx&gt;meta;meta&gt;app\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Vector databases and retrieval operations\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"emb\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Embedding model\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"idx\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Vector index\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"meta\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Metadata filters\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Application\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Embedding model to Vector index; 2, Vector index to Metadata filters; 3, Metadata filters to Application.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"emb\">\u003cdt>Embedding model \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Text to vector.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Model change.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"idx\">\u003cdt>Vector index \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Approximate search.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Low recall.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"meta\">\u003cdt>Metadata filters \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Tenant and date.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Few results.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Application \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Uses top results.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Slow queries.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-search-quality-dropped-after-an-embedding-upgrade\">Incident: Search quality dropped after an embedding upgrade\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Retrieval started returning unrelated documents after a model version change.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;select count(*) from docs where embed_model = 'v2'&quot;\npsql -c &quot;select count(*) from docs where embed_model = 'v1'&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;select count(*) from docs where embed_model = &#x27;v2&#x27;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> count\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 41000\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;select count(*) from docs where embed_model = &#x27;v1&#x27;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> count\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 960000\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>New documents were embedded with the new model while old ones kept the old vectors, so queries compared vectors from different spaces. The team re-embedded the whole collection into a new index, switched over at once and stored the model version with every vector to catch this in future.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Mixing vectors from different models in one index\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Indexes sized without overhead\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring recall and only watching latency\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Filtering that returns too few results\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No backups of the source documents\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"embeddings","text":"Embeddings"},{"id":"index-types","text":"Index types"},{"id":"sizing","text":"Sizing"},{"id":"filters-and-updates","text":"Filters and updates"},{"id":"measuring","text":"Measuring"},{"id":"incident-search-quality-dropped-after-an-embedding-upgrade","text":"Incident: Search quality dropped after an embedding upgrade"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"pgvector","u":"https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector"},{"t":"Qdrant","u":"https://github.com/qdrant/qdrant"},{"t":"Prometheus overview","u":"https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/"}],"text":"Retrieval systems find text by meaning using vectors. Operating one is a database problem with new trade-offs between speed, memory and accuracy. What you will learn Embeddings and similarity search Index types: flat, IVF and HNSW Sizing memory and disk Filtering, updates and consistency Measuring recall, latency and cost Embeddings An embedding model turns text into a vector of a few hundred to a few thousand numbers. Texts with similar meaning have vectors that are close under a measure such as cosine similarity. Changing the embedding model changes every vector, so all stored data must be re-embedded. Index types A flat index compares the query to every vector and is exact but slow at scale. Approximate indexes such as HNSW graphs or IVF clusters trade a little accuracy for much lower latency. HNSW is fast and memory heavy, IVF uses less memory and needs training on your data. Sizing A vector of 1536 floats takes about six kilobytes, so ten million vectors need roughly sixty gigabytes before the index overhead. Quantisation reduces size at some accuracy cost. Keep hot indexes in memory and plan for replicas for availability and read load. Filters and updates Most queries filter by metadata, such as tenant or date. Filtering after the search can return too few results, and filtering before can slow the search, so test your engine with realistic filters. Frequent updates and deletes may need background compaction or index rebuilds. Measuring Recall is the share of the true nearest neighbours that the index returns. Measure it against an exact search on a sample, together with latency at the 95th and 99th percentile. Tune index parameters to meet a recall target at the lowest cost, and re-test after growth. Vector databases and retrieval operations Flow in order: 1, Embedding model to Vector index; 2, Vector index to Metadata filters; 3, Metadata filters to Application. Service Data store Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Embedding model Service What it does: Text to vector. If it fails or is weak: Model change. Vector index Data store What it does: Approximate search. If it fails or is weak: Low recall. Metadata filters Step What it does: Tenant and date. If it fails or is weak: Few results. Application Service What it does: Uses top results. If it fails or is weak: Slow queries. Incident: Search quality dropped after an embedding upgrade Incident Retrieval started returning unrelated documents after a model version change. $ psql -c \"select count(*) from docs where embed_model = &#x27;v2&#x27;\" count ------- 41000 $ psql -c \"select count(*) from docs where embed_model = &#x27;v1&#x27;\" count ------- 960000 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) New documents were embedded with the new model while old ones kept the old vectors, so queries compared vectors from different spaces. The team re-embedded the whole collection into a new index, switched over at once and stored the model version with every vector to catch this in future. Common mistakes Mixing vectors from different models in one index Indexes sized without overhead Ignoring recall and only watching latency Filtering that returns too few results No backups of the source documents"},"ai-infra/rag-pipelines-in-production":{"html":"\u003cp>Retrieval augmented generation gives a model your documents at question time. Most failures come from the pipeline around the model: stale data, poor chunks and no way to tell what went wrong.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The pipeline stages\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ingestion and chunking choices\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retrieval, hybrid search and reranking\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Prompt construction and citations\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Freshness, access control and updates\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"stages\">Stages\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Documents are ingested, split into chunks, embedded and indexed. At question time the system retrieves candidate chunks, optionally reranks them, builds a prompt and calls the model, then returns the answer with sources. Each stage can fail separately, so measure each one.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"chunking\">Chunking\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Chunks that are too small lose context and chunks that are too large dilute the match and use prompt space. A few hundred tokens with some overlap and boundaries at headings is a common start. Keep metadata such as the title, section and source link with each chunk.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"retrieval\">Retrieval\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Combine vector search with keyword search, since exact names and error codes are matched better by keywords. A reranker model reorders the top candidates by relevance and often improves answer quality more than a larger model. Retrieve more than you send and keep only the best few.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"prompts-and-citations\">Prompts and citations\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Tell the model to answer only from the supplied text and to say when the answer is not there. Return the sources so users can check. Treat retrieved text as untrusted: a document can contain instructions that try to change the model behaviour.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"freshness-and-access\">Freshness and access\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Track document versions and re-index changes on a schedule or by event. Enforce access control at retrieval time with metadata filters, so a user only gets chunks they may read. Test with questions that must be answered from recent or restricted documents.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"ing&gt;ret;ret&gt;llm;llm&gt;eval\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">RAG pipelines in production\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ing\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Ingestion and chunking\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ret\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Retrieval and rerank\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"llm\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Model call\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"eval\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Evaluation\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Ingestion and chunking to Retrieval and rerank; 2, Retrieval and rerank to Model call; 3, Model call to Evaluation.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ing\">\u003cdt>Ingestion and chunking \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Prepares documents.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Poor chunks.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ret\">\u003cdt>Retrieval and rerank \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Finds context.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong passages.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"llm\">\u003cdt>Model call \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Generates answer.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignores context.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"eval\">\u003cdt>Evaluation \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Measures quality.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not measured.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-confident-answer-from-a-deleted-policy\">Incident: A confident answer from a deleted policy\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The assistant quoted a policy that had been withdrawn a month earlier.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;select doc_id, updated_at, deleted from chunks where doc_id = 'policy-17' limit 2&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;select doc_id, updated_at, deleted from chunks where doc_id = &#x27;policy-17&#x27; limit 2&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> doc_id    | updated_at | deleted\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> policy-17 | 2026-08-02 | f\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> policy-17 | 2026-08-02 | f\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The source system marked the document deleted, but the index never received the delete event. The ingestion job was changed to process deletes, a nightly reconciliation compares the index with the source and answers now show the document date.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No handling of deleted or updated documents\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Chunks without source metadata\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping evaluation and judging by a few demos\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring access control at retrieval\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Trusting retrieved text as safe instructions\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"stages","text":"Stages"},{"id":"chunking","text":"Chunking"},{"id":"retrieval","text":"Retrieval"},{"id":"prompts-and-citations","text":"Prompts and citations"},{"id":"freshness-and-access","text":"Freshness and access"},{"id":"incident-a-confident-answer-from-a-deleted-policy","text":"Incident: A confident answer from a deleted policy"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"pgvector","u":"https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector"},{"t":"Qdrant","u":"https://github.com/qdrant/qdrant"},{"t":"Langfuse","u":"https://github.com/langfuse/langfuse"}],"text":"Retrieval augmented generation gives a model your documents at question time. Most failures come from the pipeline around the model: stale data, poor chunks and no way to tell what went wrong. What you will learn The pipeline stages Ingestion and chunking choices Retrieval, hybrid search and reranking Prompt construction and citations Freshness, access control and updates Stages Documents are ingested, split into chunks, embedded and indexed. At question time the system retrieves candidate chunks, optionally reranks them, builds a prompt and calls the model, then returns the answer with sources. Each stage can fail separately, so measure each one. Chunking Chunks that are too small lose context and chunks that are too large dilute the match and use prompt space. A few hundred tokens with some overlap and boundaries at headings is a common start. Keep metadata such as the title, section and source link with each chunk. Retrieval Combine vector search with keyword search, since exact names and error codes are matched better by keywords. A reranker model reorders the top candidates by relevance and often improves answer quality more than a larger model. Retrieve more than you send and keep only the best few. Prompts and citations Tell the model to answer only from the supplied text and to say when the answer is not there. Return the sources so users can check. Treat retrieved text as untrusted: a document can contain instructions that try to change the model behaviour. Freshness and access Track document versions and re-index changes on a schedule or by event. Enforce access control at retrieval time with metadata filters, so a user only gets chunks they may read. Test with questions that must be answered from recent or restricted documents. RAG pipelines in production Flow in order: 1, Ingestion and chunking to Retrieval and rerank; 2, Retrieval and rerank to Model call; 3, Model call to Evaluation. Step Service Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Ingestion and chunking Step What it does: Prepares documents. If it fails or is weak: Poor chunks. Retrieval and rerank Service What it does: Finds context. If it fails or is weak: Wrong passages. Model call Service What it does: Generates answer. If it fails or is weak: Ignores context. Evaluation Observability What it does: Measures quality. If it fails or is weak: Not measured. Incident: A confident answer from a deleted policy Incident The assistant quoted a policy that had been withdrawn a month earlier. $ psql -c \"select doc_id, updated_at, deleted from chunks where doc_id = &#x27;policy-17&#x27; limit 2\" doc_id | updated_at | deleted policy-17 | 2026-08-02 | f policy-17 | 2026-08-02 | f (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The source system marked the document deleted, but the index never received the delete event. The ingestion job was changed to process deletes, a nightly reconciliation compares the index with the source and answers now show the document date. Common mistakes No handling of deleted or updated documents Chunks without source metadata Skipping evaluation and judging by a few demos Ignoring access control at retrieval Trusting retrieved text as safe instructions"},"ai-infra/llm-gateways-routing-caching-and-rate-limits":{"html":"\u003cp>When many teams call model APIs directly, keys leak, costs surprise and outages spread. A gateway gives one place for policy, visibility and resilience.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What a gateway does\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Routing and fallback between models and providers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Caching: exact and semantic\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rate limits, quotas and budgets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keys, logging and privacy\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"functions\">Functions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A gateway exposes one API to applications and forwards to providers or self hosted models. It holds the provider keys, authenticates callers, records usage per team and applies limits. Applications switch models by changing a name in configuration, not code.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"routing-and-fallback\">Routing and fallback\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Route simple requests to cheaper, faster models and hard ones to stronger models. Fall back to a second provider on errors or timeouts, with a retry budget so retries do not multiply load. Test that fallback models give acceptable answers, because behaviour differs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"caching\">Caching\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An exact cache returns a stored answer for an identical request, which is safe for deterministic calls. A semantic cache returns an answer for a similar request, which saves more but can return a wrong answer for a question that differs in a small, important detail. Use it only where mistakes are cheap and set a similarity threshold carefully.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"limits-and-budgets\">Limits and budgets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Limit requests and tokens per minute per team and per key, and set monthly budgets with alerts. Limit the maximum output length to stop runaway generations. Return clear errors with a retry time so clients can back off.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keys-logs-and-privacy\">Keys, logs and privacy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Never give provider keys to applications. Log metadata such as model, tokens, latency and cost for every call. Logging prompts and outputs is useful for debugging but may contain personal data, so redact it, restrict access and set a short retention period.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"app&gt;gw;gw&gt;cache;gw&gt;prov\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">LLM gateways: routing, caching and rate limits\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Applications\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"gw\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Gateway\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cache\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cache\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"prov\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Model providers\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Applications to Gateway; 2, Gateway to Cache; 3, Gateway to Model providers.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Applications \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Call one API.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Direct keys.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"gw\">\u003cdt>Gateway \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Auth, limits, routing.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Single point of failure.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cache\">\u003cdt>Cache \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Saves calls.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong semantic hit.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"prov\">\u003cdt>Model providers \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Generate text.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Outage.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-retry-storm-during-a-provider-outage\">Incident: A retry storm during a provider outage\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>When a provider slowed down, retries from every service tripled the load and the gateway fell over.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -s localhost:4000/metrics | grep -E 'requests_total|retries_total' | head -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s localhost:4000/metrics | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;requests_total|retries_total&#x27;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">gateway_requests_total{status=&quot;429&quot;} 18420\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">gateway_retries_total 52211\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Each client retried three times and the gateway retried again, multiplying traffic. A shared retry budget, exponential backoff with jitter, a circuit breaker that failed fast to the fallback provider and per team limits stopped the next incident from spreading.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Provider keys inside applications\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fallback to a model nobody tested\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Semantic cache for exact answers such as prices\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Unlimited output length\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Logging full prompts with personal data forever\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"functions","text":"Functions"},{"id":"routing-and-fallback","text":"Routing and fallback"},{"id":"caching","text":"Caching"},{"id":"limits-and-budgets","text":"Limits and budgets"},{"id":"keys-logs-and-privacy","text":"Keys, logs and privacy"},{"id":"incident-a-retry-storm-during-a-provider-outage","text":"Incident: A retry storm during a provider outage"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"LiteLLM","u":"https://github.com/BerriAI/litellm"},{"t":"vLLM","u":"https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm"},{"t":"GenAI semantic conventions","u":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/semconv/gen-ai/"}],"text":"When many teams call model APIs directly, keys leak, costs surprise and outages spread. A gateway gives one place for policy, visibility and resilience. What you will learn What a gateway does Routing and fallback between models and providers Caching: exact and semantic Rate limits, quotas and budgets Keys, logging and privacy Functions A gateway exposes one API to applications and forwards to providers or self hosted models. It holds the provider keys, authenticates callers, records usage per team and applies limits. Applications switch models by changing a name in configuration, not code. Routing and fallback Route simple requests to cheaper, faster models and hard ones to stronger models. Fall back to a second provider on errors or timeouts, with a retry budget so retries do not multiply load. Test that fallback models give acceptable answers, because behaviour differs. Caching An exact cache returns a stored answer for an identical request, which is safe for deterministic calls. A semantic cache returns an answer for a similar request, which saves more but can return a wrong answer for a question that differs in a small, important detail. Use it only where mistakes are cheap and set a similarity threshold carefully. Limits and budgets Limit requests and tokens per minute per team and per key, and set monthly budgets with alerts. Limit the maximum output length to stop runaway generations. Return clear errors with a retry time so clients can back off. Keys, logs and privacy Never give provider keys to applications. Log metadata such as model, tokens, latency and cost for every call. Logging prompts and outputs is useful for debugging but may contain personal data, so redact it, restrict access and set a short retention period. LLM gateways: routing, caching and rate limits Flow in order: 1, Applications to Gateway; 2, Gateway to Cache; 3, Gateway to Model providers. Outside party Step Data store Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Applications Outside party What it does: Call one API. If it fails or is weak: Direct keys. Gateway Step What it does: Auth, limits, routing. If it fails or is weak: Single point of failure. Cache Data store What it does: Saves calls. If it fails or is weak: Wrong semantic hit. Model providers Service What it does: Generate text. If it fails or is weak: Outage. Incident: A retry storm during a provider outage Incident When a provider slowed down, retries from every service tripled the load and the gateway fell over. $ curl -s localhost:4000/metrics | grep -E &#x27;requests_total|retries_total&#x27; | head -3 gateway_requests_total{status=\"429\"} 18420 gateway_retries_total 52211 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) Each client retried three times and the gateway retried again, multiplying traffic. A shared retry budget, exponential backoff with jitter, a circuit breaker that failed fast to the fallback provider and per team limits stopped the next incident from spreading. Common mistakes Provider keys inside applications Fallback to a model nobody tested Semantic cache for exact answers such as prices Unlimited output length Logging full prompts with personal data forever"},"ai-infra/llm-observability-evaluation-and-guardrails":{"html":"\u003cp>A model that is up can still be wrong. Observability for LLM systems must cover quality, safety and cost, not only latency and errors.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What to log and trace for each call\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Offline evaluation with test sets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Online signals and human feedback\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Guardrails: input, output and tools\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Drift: model, data and prompt changes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tracing\">Tracing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Record each step as a span: retrieval, prompt, model call and tool calls. Capture model name, token counts, latency, cost and a prompt version. A single trace should show which context was retrieved and what the model returned, so you can see why an answer was wrong.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"offline-evaluation\">Offline evaluation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Build a test set of real questions with expected answers or checks. Score with exact checks where possible, and with a grader model plus periodic human review for open answers. Run the set on every change to prompt, model or retrieval and block releases that regress.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"online-signals\">Online signals\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Track thumbs up and down, retries, answer abandonment and escalations to humans. Sample conversations for review. Combine these with cost and latency per request to find the changes that matter.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"guardrails\">Guardrails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Check inputs for prompt injection and sensitive data, and outputs for policy violations, personal data and format errors. Limit what tools a model can call and require confirmation for risky actions. Guardrails reduce risk but are not perfect, so design for the case where one fails.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"drift\">Drift\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Providers update models, documents change and users ask new things. Pin model versions where possible, keep prompts in version control, rerun the test set on a schedule and alert on shifts in refusal rate, length or cost.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"call&gt;eval;call&gt;guard;guard&gt;mon\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">LLM observability, evaluation and guardrails\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"call\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">LLM call\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"eval\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Test set and graders\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"guard\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Guardrails\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mon\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Dashboards and alerts\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, LLM call to Test set and graders; 2, LLM call to Guardrails; 3, Guardrails to Dashboards and alerts.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"call\">\u003cdt>LLM call \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Traced with metadata.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No trace.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"eval\">\u003cdt>Test set and graders \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Block regressions.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stale tests.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"guard\">\u003cdt>Guardrails \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Filter input and output.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> False negatives.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mon\">\u003cdt>Dashboards and alerts \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Quality, cost, latency.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Only latency.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-silent-change-after-a-model-update\">Incident: A silent change after a model update\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The provider updated a model alias and answers became longer and costlier overnight.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -s localhost:9090/api/v1/query --data-urlencode 'query=avg(llm_output_tokens)' | head -c 140\ncurl -s localhost:9090/api/v1/query --data-urlencode 'query=avg(llm_output_tokens offset 1d)' | head -c 140\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s localhost:9090/api/v1/query --data-urlencode \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;query=avg(llm_output_tokens)&#x27;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -c 140\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{&quot;status&quot;:&quot;success&quot;,&quot;data&quot;:{&quot;result&quot;:[{&quot;value&quot;:[1731700000,&quot;412&quot;]}]}}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s localhost:9090/api/v1/query --data-urlencode \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;query=avg(llm_output_tokens offset 1d)&#x27;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -c 140\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{&quot;status&quot;:&quot;success&quot;,&quot;data&quot;:{&quot;result&quot;:[{&quot;value&quot;:[1731613600,&quot;236&quot;]}]}}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The application used a moving alias, so behaviour changed without a deploy. The team pinned an explicit model version, added an alert on average output length and cost per request and scheduled a nightly run of the evaluation set.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Watching only latency and errors\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No test set, relying on demos\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Moving model aliases in production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Guardrails as the only defence\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Prompts edited in a dashboard with no history\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"tracing","text":"Tracing"},{"id":"offline-evaluation","text":"Offline evaluation"},{"id":"online-signals","text":"Online signals"},{"id":"guardrails","text":"Guardrails"},{"id":"drift","text":"Drift"},{"id":"incident-a-silent-change-after-a-model-update","text":"Incident: A silent change after a model update"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"GenAI semantic conventions","u":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/semconv/gen-ai/"},{"t":"Langfuse","u":"https://github.com/langfuse/langfuse"},{"t":"Prometheus overview","u":"https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/"}],"text":"A model that is up can still be wrong. Observability for LLM systems must cover quality, safety and cost, not only latency and errors. What you will learn What to log and trace for each call Offline evaluation with test sets Online signals and human feedback Guardrails: input, output and tools Drift: model, data and prompt changes Tracing Record each step as a span: retrieval, prompt, model call and tool calls. Capture model name, token counts, latency, cost and a prompt version. A single trace should show which context was retrieved and what the model returned, so you can see why an answer was wrong. Offline evaluation Build a test set of real questions with expected answers or checks. Score with exact checks where possible, and with a grader model plus periodic human review for open answers. Run the set on every change to prompt, model or retrieval and block releases that regress. Online signals Track thumbs up and down, retries, answer abandonment and escalations to humans. Sample conversations for review. Combine these with cost and latency per request to find the changes that matter. Guardrails Check inputs for prompt injection and sensitive data, and outputs for policy violations, personal data and format errors. Limit what tools a model can call and require confirmation for risky actions. Guardrails reduce risk but are not perfect, so design for the case where one fails. Drift Providers update models, documents change and users ask new things. Pin model versions where possible, keep prompts in version control, rerun the test set on a schedule and alert on shifts in refusal rate, length or cost. LLM observability, evaluation and guardrails Flow in order: 1, LLM call to Test set and graders; 2, LLM call to Guardrails; 3, Guardrails to Dashboards and alerts. Service Observability Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. LLM call Service What it does: Traced with metadata. If it fails or is weak: No trace. Test set and graders Observability What it does: Block regressions. If it fails or is weak: Stale tests. Guardrails Security What it does: Filter input and output. If it fails or is weak: False negatives. Dashboards and alerts Observability What it does: Quality, cost, latency. If it fails or is weak: Only latency. Incident: A silent change after a model update Incident The provider updated a model alias and answers became longer and costlier overnight. $ curl -s localhost:9090/api/v1/query --data-urlencode &#x27;query=avg(llm_output_tokens)&#x27; | head -c 140 {\"status\":\"success\",\"data\":{\"result\":[{\"value\":[1731700000,\"412\"]}]}} $ curl -s localhost:9090/api/v1/query --data-urlencode &#x27;query=avg(llm_output_tokens offset 1d)&#x27; | head -c 140 {\"status\":\"success\",\"data\":{\"result\":[{\"value\":[1731613600,\"236\"]}]}} (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The application used a moving alias, so behaviour changed without a deploy. The team pinned an explicit model version, added an alert on average output length and cost per request and scheduled a nightly run of the evaluation set. Common mistakes Watching only latency and errors No test set, relying on demos Moving model aliases in production Guardrails as the only defence Prompts edited in a dashboard with no history"},"ai-infra/cost-and-capacity-engineering-for-inference":{"html":"\u003cp>Inference cost grows with traffic and context length. A clear cost model lets you decide where to optimise and when running your own GPUs beats an API.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Cost per request: tokens in, tokens out\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Capacity from throughput and latency targets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Batching, quantisation and caching\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Autoscaling GPU workloads\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Hosted API versus self hosted\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cost-per-request\">Cost per request\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>API prices are quoted per million input and output tokens. Output tokens usually cost several times more. Long prompts, large retrieved context and chat histories dominate cost, so trimming them is often the biggest saving.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"capacity\">Capacity\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Throughput is measured in tokens per second per GPU, and it depends on the model size, precision, batch size and context length. Divide your peak tokens per second by the throughput of one GPU to estimate the count, add headroom for failures and test with realistic prompts, since benchmark numbers rarely match your traffic.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"optimisation\">Optimisation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Continuous batching serves many requests together and raises utilisation. Quantisation to eight or four bits cuts memory and increases speed with a small quality loss that you must measure. Prefix caching reuses the computed prompt start, which helps when many requests share a long system prompt.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"autoscaling\">Autoscaling\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>GPU nodes take minutes to start and models take more minutes to load, so reactive scaling is slow. Scale on queue depth or tokens per second, keep a warm minimum, pre-pull images and weights and schedule scale ups before known peaks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"hosted-or-self-hosted\">Hosted or self hosted\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Hosted APIs have no fixed cost and no operations, and suit low or spiky traffic. Self hosting pays off at steady high volume or for data control, but you carry staff time, spare capacity and upgrades. Compare total cost per million tokens at your real utilisation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"req&gt;batch;batch&gt;gpu;gpu&gt;cost\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Cost and capacity engineering for inference\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"req\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Requests\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"batch\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Batching and cache\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"gpu\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">GPU replicas\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cost\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cost model\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Requests to Batching and cache; 2, Batching and cache to GPU replicas; 3, GPU replicas to Cost model.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"req\">\u003cdt>Requests \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Tokens in and out.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Long context.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"batch\">\u003cdt>Batching and cache \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Raises throughput.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Cold cache.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"gpu\">\u003cdt>GPU replicas \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Tokens per second.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Slow start.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cost\">\u003cdt>Cost model \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Per million tokens.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Low utilisation.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-self-hosted-model-that-cost-more-than-the-api\">Incident: A self hosted model that cost more than the API\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A team bought GPUs to save money and found the cost per token was three times higher.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -s localhost:9090/api/v1/query --data-urlencode 'query=avg(DCGM_FI_DEV_GPU_UTIL)' | head -c 150\ncurl -s localhost:9090/api/v1/query --data-urlencode 'query=sum(increase(tokens_served_total[30d]))/1e6' | head -c 150\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s localhost:9090/api/v1/query --data-urlencode \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;query=avg(DCGM_FI_DEV_GPU_UTIL)&#x27;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -c 150\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{&quot;status&quot;:&quot;success&quot;,&quot;data&quot;:{&quot;result&quot;:[{&quot;value&quot;:[1731700000,&quot;15&quot;]}]}}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s localhost:9090/api/v1/query --data-urlencode \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;query=sum(increase(tokens_served_total[30d]))/1e6&#x27;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -c 150\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{&quot;status&quot;:&quot;success&quot;,&quot;data&quot;:{&quot;result&quot;:[{&quot;value&quot;:[1731700000,&quot;980&quot;]}]}}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Two GPUs at about three dollars an hour served 980 million tokens a month at fifteen percent utilisation, which cost more per million tokens than the hosted price. The team moved light traffic to the API, kept the GPUs for steady high volume jobs and raised utilisation with batching before buying more.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Benchmarking with unrealistic prompts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No headroom for node failures\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Self hosting at low utilisation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring context length in cost\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scaling on CPU instead of queue depth\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"cost-per-request","text":"Cost per request"},{"id":"capacity","text":"Capacity"},{"id":"optimisation","text":"Optimisation"},{"id":"autoscaling","text":"Autoscaling"},{"id":"hosted-or-self-hosted","text":"Hosted or self hosted"},{"id":"incident-a-self-hosted-model-that-cost-more-than-the-api","text":"Incident: A self hosted model that cost more than the API"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"vLLM","u":"https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm"},{"t":"KEDA","u":"https://keda.sh/docs/"},{"t":"Autoscaling workloads","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/autoscaling/"}],"text":"Inference cost grows with traffic and context length. A clear cost model lets you decide where to optimise and when running your own GPUs beats an API. What you will learn Cost per request: tokens in, tokens out Capacity from throughput and latency targets Batching, quantisation and caching Autoscaling GPU workloads Hosted API versus self hosted Cost per request API prices are quoted per million input and output tokens. Output tokens usually cost several times more. Long prompts, large retrieved context and chat histories dominate cost, so trimming them is often the biggest saving. Capacity Throughput is measured in tokens per second per GPU, and it depends on the model size, precision, batch size and context length. Divide your peak tokens per second by the throughput of one GPU to estimate the count, add headroom for failures and test with realistic prompts, since benchmark numbers rarely match your traffic. Optimisation Continuous batching serves many requests together and raises utilisation. Quantisation to eight or four bits cuts memory and increases speed with a small quality loss that you must measure. Prefix caching reuses the computed prompt start, which helps when many requests share a long system prompt. Autoscaling GPU nodes take minutes to start and models take more minutes to load, so reactive scaling is slow. Scale on queue depth or tokens per second, keep a warm minimum, pre-pull images and weights and schedule scale ups before known peaks. Hosted or self hosted Hosted APIs have no fixed cost and no operations, and suit low or spiky traffic. Self hosting pays off at steady high volume or for data control, but you carry staff time, spare capacity and upgrades. Compare total cost per million tokens at your real utilisation. Cost and capacity engineering for inference Flow in order: 1, Requests to Batching and cache; 2, Batching and cache to GPU replicas; 3, GPU replicas to Cost model. Outside party Step Service Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Requests Outside party What it does: Tokens in and out. If it fails or is weak: Long context. Batching and cache Step What it does: Raises throughput. If it fails or is weak: Cold cache. GPU replicas Service What it does: Tokens per second. If it fails or is weak: Slow start. Cost model Observability What it does: Per million tokens. If it fails or is weak: Low utilisation. Incident: A self hosted model that cost more than the API Incident A team bought GPUs to save money and found the cost per token was three times higher. $ curl -s localhost:9090/api/v1/query --data-urlencode &#x27;query=avg(DCGM_FI_DEV_GPU_UTIL)&#x27; | head -c 150 {\"status\":\"success\",\"data\":{\"result\":[{\"value\":[1731700000,\"15\"]}]}} $ curl -s localhost:9090/api/v1/query --data-urlencode &#x27;query=sum(increase(tokens_served_total[30d]))/1e6&#x27; | head -c 150 {\"status\":\"success\",\"data\":{\"result\":[{\"value\":[1731700000,\"980\"]}]}} (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) Two GPUs at about three dollars an hour served 980 million tokens a month at fifteen percent utilisation, which cost more per million tokens than the hosted price. The team moved light traffic to the API, kept the GPUs for steady high volume jobs and raised utilisation with batching before buying more. Common mistakes Benchmarking with unrealistic prompts No headroom for node failures Self hosting at low utilisation Ignoring context length in cost Scaling on CPU instead of queue depth"}});